import "lib/list.spp" as L
import "lib/text.spp" as T
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat">A presentation?</p>
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat dim">A presentation?</p>
// <p class="beat">Or a program?</p>
//%%
sieve (p : rest) = p : sieve [ x | x <- rest, x % p != 0 ]

primes =: sieve (L.from 2)
//%%
n = 12
//%%
L.take n primes
//%% [markdown]
// # Both.
//
// An **interactive document** that runs **entirely in your browser**:
// no server, no installation, and you can **edit it on the fly**.
// <span class="duo"></span>
//%%
D.slider 10 200 n
//%%
primesUpTo x = L.length (L.takeWhile (\p -> p <= x) primes)
//%%
gridSize = 100

::Dot = Dot !num !factor !seg

smallestFactor k d = if (d * d > k) 0 (if (k % d == 0) d (smallestFactor k (d + 1)))

// One dot per number; `seg` is its piece of an SVG path, computed once and shared by every frame.
dots =: [ makeDot k | k <- L.range 1 (gridSize * gridSize) ]

makeDot k =
  let i = k - 1 in
  Dot k (if (k == 1) 1 (smallestFactor k 2))
      (T.concat ["M", T.showInt (2 * (i % gridSize) + 1), " ", T.showInt (2 * (i / gridSize) + 1), "h0"])

smallPrimes =: L.filter (\p -> smallestFactor p 2 == 0) (L.range 2 gridSize)

::Sieve = { !stage : Int, !running : Bool }

sieveStart = Sieve 0 True

stages =: L.length smallPrimes + 8

sieveStep ev st = case ev
  (D.Tick t -> if (running st) (Sieve ((stage st + 1) % (stages + 1)) True) st)
  (D.Key k -> Sieve (stage st) (if (running st) False True))
  (D.Click x y -> Sieve 0 True)
  (_ -> st)

currentPrime st =
  if (stage st == 0) 1
    (if (stage st > L.length smallPrimes) (gridSize + 1) (L.nth (stage st - 1) smallPrimes))

// 0 = green (not crossed out), 1 = grey, 2 = light grey (just crossed out), 3 = yellow (the current prime)
dotState p d = case d
  (Dot k f seg ->
     if (k == 1) 1
       (if (k == p) 3
         (if (f == 0) 0
           (if (f == p) 2 (if (f < p) 1 0)))))

dotSeg d = case d (Dot k f seg -> seg)

dotPath p state colour =
  T.concat ["<path stroke='", colour, "' stroke-width='1.45' stroke-linecap='round' fill='none' d='",
            T.concat (L.map dotSeg (L.filter (\d -> dotState p d == state) dots)), "'/>"]

sieveView st =
  let p = currentPrime st, w = T.showInt (2 * gridSize) in
  D.markdown (T.concat
    ["<svg class='sieve' viewBox='0 0 ", w, " ", w, "'>",
     dotPath p 0 "#22c55e", dotPath p 1 "#4b5563", dotPath p 2 "#9ca3af", dotPath p 3 "#facc15",
     "</svg><p class='figcap'>Sieve of Eratosthenes, 10,000 numbers</p>"])
//%%
D.column [D.lineChart [ primesUpTo x | x <- L.range 2 n ],
          D.markdown "<p class='figcap'>Number of primes up to <i>n</i></p>"]
//%%
D.animated 400 sieveStart sieveStep sieveView
//%% [markdown]
// ## What just happened?
//
// - Every cell is a small program, **compiled when you change it** and linked
//   into the running session; only the cells that depend on it run again.
// - The compiler, the linker and the typechecker are **themselves bytecode**,
//   running on **one small VM** in WebAssembly, in this browser tab.
// - The infinite list `primes` is computed **lazily**: only as far as a cell asks.
//
// <p class="hint">Press <b>C</b> to show the code, change it, and watch the slide follow.</p>
//%%
D.table ["Step, per cell", "Runs where", "Written in"]
  [ ["Sapl+ preprocessor (records, ZF, patterns)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Typechecker", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Compiler gen1 (to bytecode)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Linker, loading into the session", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["The VM itself", "this tab (WebAssembly)", "C/C++"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## Where it started: IFL 2016
//
// <div class="twocol">
// <div>
// <p><em>A Portable VM-based Implementation Platform for Non-strict Functional Programming Languages</em></p>
// <ul>
// <li>A small VM for lazy functional languages: the <b>JMVM</b>, about <b>1,500 lines of C</b></li>
// <li>A small core language, <b>Sapl</b>, with a compiler of about <b>1,000 lines of Amanda</b></li>
// <li>Runs in the <b>browser</b>, and stays within a <b>factor of 5</b> of Clean and Haskell</li>
// </ul>
// <p class="hint">A prototype. The question in July 2026: how far can one person take it, with LLMs?</p>
// </div>
// <img src="afscheid/paper_first_page.png" alt="First page of the IFL 2016 paper">
// </div>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The wild part of the experiment
//
// - **All code since July was written by language models.** I only wrote prompts.
// - The models: **Claude Opus** and **Claude Sonnet**, and at times **Gemini Flash**, **Hydrogen** and **Argon**.
// - My part: the direction, the questions, and deciding what to keep.
//
// <p class="hint">First what is there and how fast it is; the last part of this talk is about how it was built with LLMs.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why was this a good starting point?
//
// Two choices in the 2016 prototype turned out to matter far more than we knew at the time:
//
// 1. **A structured intermediate form** between the language and the VM: what we now call **Retag**.
// 2. **Laziness isolated in two instructions**, `eval` and `jmpt`. The rest of the VM is a plain,
//    eager stack machine.
//
// <p class="hint">We only found out later how much of the follow-up work rests on these two choices.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## 1. Retag: laziness made explicit
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// **Sapl** (the `primes` benchmark)
//
// ```
// filter p xs = case xs (Nil -> Nil)
//   (Cons a as -> if (p a)
//      (Cons a (filter p as))
//      (filter p as))
//
// from a = Cons a (from (a+1))
//
// sieve xs = case xs nomatch
//   (Cons a as ->
//      Cons a (sieve (filter (nmz a) as)))
//
// primes = sieve (from 2)
// ```
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// **Retag** (after strictness analysis and lifting)
//
// ```
// filter p !xs = case xs (Nil -> Nil)
//   (Cons a as -> if (eval(p a))
//      (Cons a (thunk (filter p as)))
//      (filter p (eval(as))))
//
// from a = Cons (eval(a))
//   (thunk (from (thunk (from_1 a))))
//
// sieve !xs = case xs (Nil -> nomatch)
//   (Cons a as -> Cons a (thunk
//      (sieve (thunk (filter (closure (nmz a)) as)))))
//
// primes = sieve (from 2)
// ```
//
// </div>
// </div>
//
// Every decision about laziness is **visible in the code**: `eval` (evaluate now), `thunk` (later),
// `closure` (partial application), `!` (strict argument). A backend only has to follow it.
//%% [markdown]
// ## 2. Laziness isolated: `eval` and `jmpt`
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// - **`eval`**: the *only* place where a thunk is reduced. It loops on the VM's own control
//   stack, so deep laziness never touches the C stack.
// - **`jmpt`**: a jump table on the constructor of an evaluated value: `case` in one instruction.
// - Everything else (arithmetic, comparisons, calls, building nodes) is **eager** and knows
//   nothing about laziness.
//
// <p class="hint">The JMVM code of <code>sieve</code>, just to show it:</p>
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// ```
// 22  load 0               || sieve_lazy
//     eval
//     store 0
// 23  load 0               || sieve
//     jmpt 2 24 25
// 24  push "sieve_Nil"     || sieve_Nil
//     debug 2
//     return 1
// 25  load 1               || sieve_Cons
//     load 0
//     pushfunc 2 12        || nmz_lazy
//     create 2 7
//     pushfunc 2 15        || filter_lazy
//     create 3 3
//     pushfunc 1 22        || sieve_lazy
//     create 2 3
//     load 0
//     ccreatet 2 11 5 1
//     return 3
// ```
//
// </div>
// </div>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The key turned out to be Retag, not the VM
//
// Almost everything built since starts from **Retag**, not from the bytecode:
//
// - **Native code**: Retag to C (`sapl2c`), roughly as fast as GHC `-O2`.
// - **Clean on the JMVM**: ABC decompiled back to Retag (`abc2retag`).
// - **Haskell lite**, Sapl+ and Core Sapl: all lower to the same Retag.
// - **Linking modules** and the **optimiser** work on Retag.
//
// <p class="beat">In Clean, ABC code is the hub.<br>Here it is one level higher.</p>
//
// <p class="hint">Retag was never designed as an interface: in 2016 it only existed to explain the compiler in the paper.</p>
//
// <p class="hint">Same clang, two inputs: C from bytecode stayed well behind Clean native; C from Retag is roughly as fast.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Four months, step by step
//%%
D.table ["When (2026)", "Step"]
  [ ["17 July", "Start from the 2016 prototype: VM in C, compiler in Amanda"],
    ["12 August", "The compiler, now written in Sapl, compiles itself: self-hosting"],
    ["25-26 September", "It becomes the production compiler; the first notebook"],
    ["7-11 October", "REPL and tools in Sapl, not in the VM; binary .jvb format"],
    ["14 October", "A typechecker on every compile"],
    ["23 October", "Notebooks complete: forms, interactive cells, values kept"],
    ["1-8 November", "Typed Haskell anchor, superinstructions, Haskell lite, Clean via ABC"],
    ["11-13 November", "Native code: Retag to C"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## Self-hosting: the compiler compiles itself
//
// - From **19 July** the compiler was rewritten **in Sapl itself** (gen1), next to the Amanda original.
// - **12 August**: the fixpoint. gen1 compiles its own source to exactly the same bytecode.
// - **25-26 September**: gen1 becomes the production compiler; the code it generates is as fast as or
//   faster than the old compiler's on every benchmark.
// - **1 November**: a **typed Haskell version**, generated from gen1, takes over as the independent
//   anchor. The Amanda compiler is frozen.
//
// <p class="hint">From then on, all language work happens in Sapl, on the VM, and can be checked byte for byte.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The turning point: "Can we do something useful with it?"
//
// - **25 September**: an overview of all REPLs ends with a chapter *Applications*
//   (teaching, a Jupyter-like kernel, playgrounds in the browser, exploring data).
// - **That evening**: a plan for *notebooks, libraries and qualified imports*.
// - **26 September**: the first Sapl notebook, with libraries for statistics, plots and CSV.
//
// <p class="beat sm">You are looking at the result!</p>
// <span class="duo"></span>
//%%
::Pt3 = Pt3 !x !y !z
::Edge = Edge !from !to

::Cube = { !angleX : Float, !angleY : Float, !spinning : Bool }

cubeStart = Cube 0.4 0.6 True

cubeCorners =:
  let r = [0.0 - 0.55, 0.55] in
  [Pt3 x y z | x <- r, y <- r, z <- r]

cubeEdges =:
  let r = [0.0 - 0.55, 0.55] in
  L.concat
    [[Edge (Pt3 (0.0 - 0.55) a b) (Pt3 0.55 a b) | a <- r, b <- r],
     [Edge (Pt3 a (0.0 - 0.55) b) (Pt3 a 0.55 b) | a <- r, b <- r],
     [Edge (Pt3 a b (0.0 - 0.55)) (Pt3 a b 0.55) | a <- r, b <- r]]

rotX phi p = case p (Pt3 x y z -> Pt3 x (cos phi * y - sin phi * z) (sin phi * y + cos phi * z))

rotY phi p = case p (Pt3 x y z -> Pt3 (cos phi * x + sin phi * z) y (cos phi * z - sin phi * x))

project p = case p (Pt3 x y z -> G.Pt (1.6 * x / (z + 2.4)) (1.6 * y / (z + 2.4)))

corner c p = project (rotX (angleX c) (rotY (angleY c) p))

drawEdge c e = case e (Edge p q -> G.GraphPolyLine 11 [corner c p, corner c q])

dot col p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.GraphDisc col (G.Pt (x - 0.012) (y - 0.012)) (G.Pt (x + 0.012) (y + 0.012)))

cubeStep ev c = case ev
  (D.Tick t -> if (spinning c) (Cube (angleX c + 0.025) (angleY c + 0.038) True) c)
  (D.Click x y -> Cube (0.0 - y * 1.5) (x * 1.5) (spinning c))
  (D.Key k -> Cube (angleX c) (angleY c) (if (spinning c) False True))
  (_ -> c)

cubeView c = D.picture (L.append (L.map (drawEdge c) cubeEdges) (L.map (\p -> dot 14 (corner c p)) cubeCorners))
//%%
::Figure = { !fx : Int, !fy : Int, !phase : Float }

figStart = Figure 3 2 0.0

figStep ev f = case ev
  (D.Tick t -> Figure (fx f) (fy f) (phase f + 0.04))
  (D.Click x y -> Figure (fy f) (fx f + 1) (phase f))
  (_ -> f)

lissajous f =
  [G.GraphPolyLine 9 [ G.Pt (0.9 * sin (itof (fx f) * t + phase f)) (0.9 * sin (itof (fy f) * t))
                     | t <- L.map (\k -> itof k * 0.0315) (L.range 0 200) ]]

figView f = D.picture (lissajous f)
//%%
D.animated 40 cubeStart cubeStep cubeView
//%%
D.animated 40 figStart figStep figView
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why notebooks suit Sapl so well
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// **Classic notebooks (Jupyter, Python)**
//
// - **Hidden mutable state**: a result depends on the order in which cells happened to run.
// - Change something early on: **Restart & Run All**.
// - Large or infinite data is computed **up front**, whether you look at it or not.
// - Widgets: **callbacks that change global state**.
// - A **kernel on a server** or in a container; a restart loses every result.
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// **Sapl notebooks**
//
// - **Pure**: a cell is a function of its inputs; a change reruns only the cells that depend on it.
// - **Lazy**: a definition costs nothing until needed; infinite lists are normal.
// - **Shared**: a `=:` value is computed once, for every cell.
// - **Interactive cells: the Elm architecture**: a model, a pure `update`, a `view`.
// - **In the tab**: a cell in **6–8&nbsp;ms**, no server, offline; values survive a reload.
//
// </div>
// </div>
//
// <p class="hint">The VM runs in a Web Worker, so a long computation never freezes the page. Details: the notebooks factsheet.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The consequence: tools in Sapl, not in the VM
//
// - **7-8 October**: the REPL becomes **one Sapl program** instead of three hosts in C++, Python and
//   JavaScript. The VM only offers a few primitives.
// - **9-11 October**: a **binary load format** (`.jvb`): the VM no longer reads text at all.
// - The compiler, linker, typechecker and REPL all run as **bytecode**: unchanged in the browser,
//   natively, and on a server.
//%%
D.table ["Effect of .jvb", "Before", "After"]
  [ ["Start the REPL", "~117 ms", "~6 ms"],
    ["Compile a small program", "~40 ms", "~12 ms"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="hint">A rule that stuck: extend the VM only for a real, frequent need without alternative;
// everything else becomes a Sapl module.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Then: more languages and backends, all through Retag
//
// - <span class="dropped">**MicroHs**, a real Haskell compiler, bootstrapped on the JMVM: hard going, not continued.</span>
// - **Haskell lite** in its place: type classes, records and monads, translated to Sapl+, running in the browser.
// - **Clean**: ABC code from the Clean compiler, decompiled back to Retag (`abc2retag`).
// - **Native code**: Retag to C (`sapl2c`), a first prototype.
//
// <p class="hint">Each new frontend or backend only had to meet Retag, not every other part.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The whole picture
//
// <img class="overview" src="top2026/ecosystem.svg" alt="Overview of the ecosystem: frontends, Retag, backends and tools">
//
// <p class="hint">Not to explain, just to show what is there. Details: the factsheets.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## How fast is the interpreter?
//%%
D.table ["JMVM interpreter vs. (Apple M4)", "Mean", "Per program"]
  [ ["Clean native", "2.7× slower", "1.8–4.6×"],
    ["GHC -O2 (without full laziness)", "3.2× slower", "1.5–5.1×"],
    ["Clean's ABC interpreter, native", "1.25× slower", "0.7–1.7×"],
    ["Itself, in the browser (WebAssembly, Chrome)", "1.47× slower", ""],
    ["The compiler compiling itself: GHC -O2", "2.0× slower", "598 vs. 295 ms"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - The 2016 target was **within a factor of 5** of Clean and Haskell. Natively it is now about **3×** on average,
//   but it depends a lot on the program: from 1.5× to 5×.
// - In the browser it loses **less than 1.5×** against itself natively.
//
// <p class="hint">Geometric means over the same 12 benchmarks (sort left out: Clean 3.1 native ARM64 gives a wrong result); best of 15 runs. Clean native in a Linux VM on the same machine (9 Nov). GHC (18 Nov): literal ports of the Sapl programs, and -fno-full-laziness, because otherwise GHC lifts the work the benchmark repeats out of its loop (parsetest: 12× less work). Sources: experiments/clean_vergelijking/meting_2026-11-09 and meting_2026-11-18.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Where the interpreter's speed came from
//%%
D.table ["Improvement", "When", "Gain", "Whose idea"]
  [ ["Strictness analysis: finds the ! automatically", "August", "without it slower: fib 6.9×, rest ≤1.9×", "classic (Mycroft)"],
    ["musttail dispatch: every instruction its own function", "24 Oct", "the compiler -8.6%, fib -54% (*)", "mine: tail calls vs. goto?"],
    ["Instruction fusion (superinstructions)", "1 Nov", "-15% native, -13% Chrome, -14% Safari", "mine: does fusion pay off?"],
    ["case as a real jump instead of a jump table", "2 Nov", "the compiler -25%, knights -38%", "the model, from hardware counters"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - **Net, against VM and compiler of 17 July**: **1.4× faster** on average, from **1.0×** (fib) to **2.1×** (sprimes).
// - (*) **Not a straight climb**: the growing VM slowed its goto loop (fib 0.24 s in July, 0.55 s in October); musttail mainly won that back.
//
// <p class="hint">Each gain measured against the VM of that moment. Apple M4, native (V8 prefers the switch form). Goto today: fib 583 ms, musttail 253 ms. July: 11 of 13 benchmarks (twice changed, eval2 new), same output.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Looking inside the processor: why `case` stalled
//
// My question was simply: *"measure very precisely whether there is anything left to gain in the VM."*
// I had no idea in which direction; the model came with the approach and the fix.
//
// - **Hardware counters** (the processor's own performance counters) on the compiler compiling itself.
// - Not branch mispredictions, not cache misses: **93% of the gap** with Clean's interpreter was
//   the processor **waiting for operands**.
// - The cause: `case` looked up its branch in a jump table, a chain of **four memory reads** that
//   every following instruction had to wait for.
// - The fix: a real, predictable conditional jump for the first two constructors. **A few lines**,
//   no new instruction, no change to the compiler.
//
// <p class="hint">I could not have found this myself, at least not without a deep study of the subject first.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The surprise: native code through Retag
//
// **sapl2c**: Retag to C, one C function per Sapl function, compiled with `clang -O2`.
// Built in **three days** (11-13 November): ~1,150 lines of Python (or ~1,800 of Sapl)
// plus an 818-line C runtime.
//
// - A **first prototype**, very experimental and hardly tried on real programs yet.
// - Even so: on the benchmarks and on the compiler compiling itself, **roughly as fast as
//   Clean native and GHC `-O2`**, and about **3× faster** than the interpreter.
//
// <p class="beat sm">I really had not expected this.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why it works: Retag, again
//
// - The same clang on two inputs: C **from bytecode** (`jmvm2c`) and C **from Retag** (`sapl2c`).
//   From Retag the C is much smaller and builds about **10× faster**, and the code is much faster.
// - From Retag, clang sees **whole functions with named variables**: a classic C compiler turns out
//   to be a good code generator for a lazy functional language.
// - Laziness is already explicit (`eval`, `thunk`), and gen1 already did strictness and lifting.
// - Plus a few runtime choices: a **cache-friendly growing heap**, values returned in two registers,
//   and every tail call a real jump.
//%% [markdown]
// ## How could one person build this?
//
//%%
D.table ["Since 17 July 2026, all written by models", "About"]
  [ ["Commits", "1,200, plus 200 merged branches"],
    ["Sapl / Sapl+ (toolchain 40k, libraries 16k, experiments and tests 34k)", "90,000 lines"],
    ["VM in C/C++, written (plus generated dispatch)", "6,600 lines"],
    ["Design documents and reports", "150 documents"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - **A small VM**: 1,500 lines in 2016. New: `pack`/`unpack`, the binary loader, live linking, reflection.
// - A **self-hosting compiler** (gen1), anchored by a typed Haskell version.
// - One intermediate form, **Retag**, shared by every route: Sapl, Haskell lite and Clean.
// - **LLMs** made experiments cheap: five backends were built and *measured*, instead of argued about
//   (JMVM bytecode, bytecode-to-Wasm, bytecode-to-C, Retag-to-ABC, and Retag-to-C).
//%% [markdown]
// ## Two phases
//%%
D.table ["", "17 July – mid September", "Mid September – now"]
  [ ["Way of working", "Exploring: can it be done at all?", "Goal-directed: plan, branch, regression, measure"],
    ["Commits", "about 500", "about 700"],
    ["Merged branches", "a few dozen", "about 200"],
    ["Plan documents", "a handful", "about 50"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - The turning point: **self-hosting gave oracles** (the fixpoint, the full regression).
//   From then on code generation worked much better, and steps could be much larger.
// - It was also the point where I had **learned to work with the models**: that took about two months.
// - The exploring phase was **necessary**: that is how research works, and without it the oracles
//   would not have existed. Most dead ends on the next slide come from it.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Dead ends, and why that was fine
//%%
D.table ["Experiment", "What happened", "Replaced by"]
  [ ["Amanda-to-Sapl translator (August)", "Partly worked; a hang in the full bridge was never found", "The compiler written from scratch in Sapl"],
    ["A JIT to WebAssembly (September)", "Correct, but slower than the warm interpreter", "The plain interpreter in wasm"],
    ["Bytecode to C, jmvm2c (November)", "Well behind Clean native, very long builds", "Retag to C, sapl2c"],
    ["Whole-program Retag optimiser", "Doubled the compiler, four subtle bugs", "One local rule in gen1"],
    ["MicroHs on the JMVM (November)", "Bootstrapped, but heavy and server-bound", "Haskell lite, in the browser"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - Starting again **from scratch** usually went better than repairing the first attempt.
// - With LLMs, **throwing work away is cheap**: you did not spend weeks typing it yourself.
//%% [markdown]
// ## The real insight: verification, not speed
//
// LLM-written code became trustworthy through **oracles that check byte for byte**:
//
// - the **fixpoint** of gen1: the compiler must reproduce itself exactly;
// - the **typed Haskell version** as an independent anchor;
// - **two generators** (Python and Sapl) that must emit the same C;
// - the **full regression** on every change, and the documents first.
//
// <p class="hint">What went wrong, honestly: an agent reported 38 ms for the compiler compiling itself.
// It was an aborted run; the fixpoint check caught it.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Who knew what?
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// **The model knew what I could never know**
//
// - Hardware counters showed the CPU *waiting* on the `jmpt` jump table: one change, the compiler **−25%**.
//   I had only asked to look.
// - Retag to C: correct and fast within **days**, using C and Clang knowledge I do not have.
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// **The expert knew where to go**
//
// - The starting point: Retag, `eval` and `jmpt`.
// - The questions: would instruction fusion and tail calls beat computed goto? Both paid off.
// - Clean's interpreter to wasm: the model chose `musttail`; I asked for *switch*, which is better in the browser.
// - When a number is too good, or a path is a dead end.
//
// </div>
// </div>
//
// <p class="beat sm">Research with LLMs is a new kind of science.<br>Experience is a huge advantage; working with LLMs has to be learned too.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The models
//
// - **Claude Opus** gave by far the best results.
// - I let the models **check each other's work**: one builds, another reviews
//   (these slides were reviewed by Argon and by Claude).
// - The Gemini models (Google) regularly **missed the point completely**.
//   The aborted 38 ms run came from one of them.
//
// <p class="hint">Which model writes matters less than what checks it: the oracles of the previous slides.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Measured so far (first prototypes)
//%%
D.table ["Apple M4, native", "Mean", "Per program"]
  [ ["JMVM interpreter and compiler now vs. 17 July", "1.4× faster", "0.47–1.00×"],
    ["Sapl via Retag to C (sapl2c) vs. Clean native", "1.2× faster", "0.50–1.33×"],
    ["Sapl via Retag to C vs. GHC -O2", "as fast", "0.64–1.65×"],
    ["Clean via ABC, Retag and C vs. Clean native", "as fast", "0.58–1.75×"],
    ["gen1 ported to Clean, compiling itself, via C vs. Clean native", "1.43× slower", "234 vs. 163 ms"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - **Small benchmarks**: C from Retag keeps up with Clean's `cg` and with GHC; it wins where much is allocated.
// - **A large typed program**: `cg` wins, probably because Retag is untyped and boxes values that Clean keeps unboxed.
//
// <p class="hint">Geometric means over 12 benchmarks; per program: time ratio, lower is faster. gen1 is the Sapl compiler. Clean native in a Linux VM (noise up to 15–20%); GHC without full laziness. Source: experiments/clean_vergelijking/meting_2026-11-18.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Demo: the Clean sandbox, extended
//
// <img class="overview" src="top2026/sandbox.svg" alt="Clean and Sapl, ABC code and Retag, the ABC interpreter and the JMVM, connected by abc2retag and retag2abc">
//
// - TOP's **Clean sandbox**, with a second language (**Sapl**) and a second interpreter (the **JMVM**).
// - `abc2retag` and `retag2abc` connect ABC code and Retag **in both directions**: every language reaches every interpreter.
// - All 13 benchmarks in both languages give the **same output on both interpreters**.
//
// <p class="hint"><a href="clean-sandbox/" target="_blank">janmartinjansen.github.io/websapl/clean-sandbox</a> · retag2abc is sapl2abc.cfp in the code, itself written in Sapl.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## In the browser: which combination is fastest?
//
// <table class="speed">
// <tr><th>Time (lower is faster)</th><th>Chrome<br><span>V8</span></th><th>Safari<br><span>JavaScriptCore</span></th><th>Firefox<br><span>SpiderMonkey</span></th></tr>
// <tr><td>ABC interpreter (as shipped)</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.15</td><td class="best">0.75</td></tr>
// <tr><td>ABC, globals to locals</td><td class="best">0.65</td><td>1.20</td><td>1.52</td></tr>
// <tr><td>ABC, C interpreter via Emscripten</td><td>0.71</td><td>1.51</td><td>1.82</td></tr>
// <tr><td>JMVM (via abc2retag)</td><td>0.78</td><td class="best">0.76</td><td>1.12</td></tr>
// </table>
//
// - **The best build differs per engine**: locals in Chrome, the shipped interpreter in Firefox, the JMVM in Safari (and so on every iPad).
// - Locals and Emscripten only pay off in **V8**; in SpiderMonkey they **double** the time.
// - The JMVM depends least on the browser: **0.76–1.12**, against 0.65–1.82 for the ABC builds.
//
// <p class="hint">Chrome with the shipped abc-interpreter.wasm = 1.00. Apple M4, warm; the 14 programs compiled by Clean (clm -optabc), the JMVM runs the same ABC via abc2retag; geometric mean.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What could this mean for Clean?
//
// Questions rather than lessons:
//
// 1. **A faster ABC interpreter in the browser** is possible by choosing per browser engine:
//    with locals it is a third faster in Chrome, the same in Safari, but twice as slow in Firefox.
//    Clean's own C interpreter via Emscripten does slightly worse than locals everywhere.
// 2. **A somewhat higher-level intermediate form?** ABC is stack code, like JMVM bytecode.
//    Here the level of the intermediate form mattered more than the backend, and `abc2retag`
//    shows the structure can be recovered from ABC. It may be worth a look.
// 3. **A classic C compiler as code generator?** Via Retag it worked surprisingly well,
//    also for Clean code via ABC. But `sapl2c` is a very early prototype, and types are lost on the way.
// 4. **Developing with LLMs**: the oracles and the way of working carry over to any compiler project.
//%% [markdown]
// ## What has not been measured
//
// - A real **iTasks** application: speed, and **compile time**.
// - **Uniqueness and arrays**, records, `ccall`, dynamics.
// - The native route in **WebAssembly**, and graph compatibility with the ABC interpreter.
// - A real **iPad**; Windows.
